  {
    "id": 299,
    "post": 782,
    "parent": 298,
    "author": 28093736,
    "author_name": "kidinjurylaw",
    "author_url": "http://kidinjurylaw.wordpress.com",
    "date": "2013-10-28T09:30:13",
    "date_gmt": "2013-10-28T13:30:13",
    "content": {
      "rendered": "<p>It is not acceptable. The Georgia legislature and the Georgia Court of Appeals protect school officials from exposure to civil liability. It is, in my opinion, an abuse of power for one branch of the State to shield another branch from liability for negligent acts. The State makes education mandatory for our children and there should be a corresponding responsibility for safeguarding our children while they are at school. </p>\n<p>And you are absolutely correct, if your son showed up at school with bruises and fingerprints, it is highly likely DFACS would receive a call from school officials and you would be investigated. So it&#8217;s a double standard. </p>\n<p>I&#8217;m so sorry your son was hurt. If enough parents band together and vote for representatives and Judges that would address this injustice, change is possible. </p>\n<p>Thank you for sharing your comment.</p>\n<p>Attorney Pete Pearson<br />\n<a href=\"http://petepearsonlaw.com\" title=\"PEARSON LAW FIRM website\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\"></a></p>\n"
    },
    "link": "https://kidinjurylaw.com/2013/04/08/public-school-injury-law-in-georgia/#comment-299",
